# Local runbook

1. Use Node >=22.18 and <25. This continuation tested Node 24.15.0. Node 22 compatibility still needs a separate runtime check.
2. `npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund` installs the integrity-locked dependencies. No lifecycle scripts are required.
3. Run `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm test`, `npm run capability:validate`, `npm run discovery:build`.
4. Before running `npm start`, check the proposed port against `D:\MASTER.env` without exposing other environment values. No persistent dev server was started during this continuation; tests bind ephemeral loopback ports.
5. Leave `STUB_MODE=1`, `LISTED=false`. `/health` reports `paymentsLive:false`. Production execution is refused regardless of stub configuration. No environment variable enables live payments in this build.

The sandbox used here denied writes to the normal npm cache. Dependencies were copied read-only from the existing local cache into `.local/npm-cache` and installed offline. No network or permission restriction was bypassed. That cache and workstation helper scripts are ignored and are not required on another machine.

## Safe local invocation

`GET /tools/game_launch_kit` returns a 402 with a hypothetical payment requirement. Retry locally with `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE: stub-ok` to obtain a visibly marked stub result. This is a payment-flow demonstration, not a commercial offer. HTTP POST input is JSON; GET query input is retained for compatibility and does not coerce numeric types. MCP uses `_meta["x402/payment"]` and `_meta["x402/idempotency-key"]`; payment data is not merged into handler arguments.

`DISABLED_CAPABILITIES` is a comma-separated deny list loaded at startup. Registry statuses and discovery flags are immutable deployment configuration. Restart after changing configuration. No unauthenticated operator mutation endpoint exists. `/tools`, `/capabilities` and MCP `tools/list` are empty with the default registry. `robots.txt` and response headers deny indexing.

## Persistence setup for local recovery testing

Construct `SqliteLedger(path)` and inject it as `createApp(config, { ledger })`. For telemetry, construct `SqliteEventSink(path)` and `new Telemetry(event => sink.write(event), operatorManagedSalt)`, then inject `telemetry`. Use distinct ledger and telemetry database files. Call `.close()` on each database on shutdown. Files are ignored by Git; protect their access because completed response bodies can contain user data.

The default MemoryLedger is explicitly nondurable. Never substitute SQLite in a Vercel temporary directory for distributed payment safety. Live enablement requires a shared database with atomic nonce uniqueness, intent binding, cost reservations, durable completion and reconciliation tests.

## Ambiguous operation recovery

On a pending or settlement-unknown row, do not delete it or automatically retry settlement. Reconcile the facilitator and chain using approved read-only tools in a separately authorized session. Check whether the handler performed an external side effect and whether settlement occurred. Retain the tombstone. Restoring service requires a reviewed operator reconciliation procedure; this build deliberately has no unsafe public reset endpoint. A timeout does not prove the remote operation did not finish.

Back up the ledger before upgrades. Preserve its nonce tombstones across rollback. Price/version changes can invalidate a cached request fingerprint; keep old versions available for reconciliation. Do not use analytics as the source of truth for payment correctness. Event retention may be pruned with `SqliteEventSink.prune`; payment replay records have no automatic expiry. Plan a reviewed retention policy before production.

## Release restrictions

Do not run deployment, DNS, listing, paid upstream, faucet, wallet funding, or live-payment commands as part of these checks. No production deployment command is provided. Git branch and commits are blocked in the current sandbox; see BUILD-STATUS.md and PR-DRAFT.md for a reviewable handoff.
